# Cone Ten - a kiln firing, one night, and a decision that ruins the pots if taken early.
# Written for the library as a SHORT and DENSE one: 37 of the 42 stories before it sit between 10 and 19 scenes,
# so this is eight scenes with more per scene - a different reading shape rather than a different subject.

title: Cone Ten
start: start

== start ==
bg workshop_bench
music room_tone
You pack on the Thursday and light it at nine on the Friday night, and by ten the kiln is at a hundred
degrees and the smoke coming off the top of the chimney is white and wet and smells of the damp in the
bricks, which is the smell of the first four hours and the reason you do not go to bed.
The kiln is a forty-cubic-foot updraught and it is older than you are, and the firing takes fourteen
hours and about a ton and a half of wood, and you have fired it a hundred and sixty times and lost
work on eleven of them.
-> the_ramp

== the_ramp ==
There are two ways up. The slow one is a hundred degrees an hour to six hundred and then open it up,
which takes eleven hours to get to temperature and is kind to everything in the stack, including the
four big jars that are the whole reason for the firing.
The fast one is to open it up at four hundred, which saves three hours, and which can craze a glaze or
take the belly out of a big pot, and which you have done twice in nine years and got away with both.
The forecast is for the wind to get up at four in the morning and for rain on the Saturday, which
matters because the kiln has no roof over the far side of it.
* Slow ramp. Eleven hours and no heroics. -> slow
* Fast ramp. You have got away with it twice and the jars are dry. -> fast
* Split it: slow to six hundred and then push harder than usual. -> split

== slow ==
You feed it small and often and you sit on the crate by the door in the dark with the door open,
and the kiln goes up a hundred an hour like a train keeping time, and at six hundred the firebox is
clean and the flame at the top of the chimney is orange and steady.
The pots will be all right. You know this at about two in the morning, and knowing it is the whole
difference between the two ways up: the slow one is not about the pots, it is about the eleven hours
in which nothing can go wrong that you cannot fix with a stick of wood.
~ slow 1
-> the_wind

== fast ==
You open it up at four hundred and the kiln takes it, and by midnight it is at eight hundred and the
bricks on the outside of the arch are too hot to put a hand flat on, and there is a noise in the
firebox that a kiln makes when it is going well.
You are three hours ahead. You spend them standing in the doorway looking at the arch and listening,
which is what a potter does with three hours he has bought with the risk of his best four jars.
-> the_wind

== split ==
You take it slow to six hundred and then push it, which is a compromise and which is the one thing the
kiln does not do well, because the arch has a crack in the third course from the left and it has been
stable for nineteen years at one rate and it does not like a change.
It does not like a change now either. At half past two there is a click you have heard once before,
and you go round the back with a torch and there is a hairline in the third course that was not there
at the start, and you stand in the dark with the torch on it and do the arithmetic about the four jars.
-> the_wind

== the_wind ==
The wind gets up at four exactly as forecast, and it comes round the far side of the kiln where there
is no roof, and at four twenty it starts taking heat off the stack: the chimney stops drawing properly
and the flame goes from orange to grey and the kiln's rate drops by a third in ten minutes.
This happens once or twice a year and there is a way to handle it, which is to put the boards up on
the windward side and feed harder and hold the rate, and there is another way, which is to let it drop
and take the firing up to sixteen hours and get to temperature on the Saturday evening instead.
* Boards up, feed harder, hold the rate. -> boards
* Let it drop and finish on Saturday evening. -> let_it_drop
* Shut the firebox right down, soak it at eight hundred for three hours, and finish late. -> soak

== boards ==
You are out in the dark with the boards and the cold coming sideways and you get the windward side
covered in twenty minutes with your hands going numb, and you feed it heavier than you would ever
feed it in still air, and the rate comes back up over forty minutes and holds.
The four jars come through it. You will not know that until the following Friday, and you know it
anyway at half past four in the morning, which is a thing about firing that nobody tells you: the
kiln is honest, and if you hold the rate it will hold the temperature, and the feeling you get when
the firebox answers you is the whole reason you have not bought an electric one.
-> ending_held

== let_it_drop ==
You let it drop and you do not fight it, and you spend the rest of the night in the doorway with your
back against the frame, feeding it steady and taking the temperature where it goes rather than where
you want it, and at nine in the morning the kiln is at eleven hundred and rising and you have been
awake for twenty-six hours.
You finish at half past seven on the Saturday evening, sixteen and a half hours after you lit it, and
you are too tired to be pleased and too old to be clever about it, and you brick up the spyhole and go
to bed while it is still at temperature behind you.
if slow >= 1 -> the_cool_slow
-> the_cool

== soak ==
You shut the firebox down at quarter to five in the morning with the kiln at eight hundred and you hold
it there for three hours, which is a soak and which is a thing people do for glazes that need it, and
then you take it up through the last four hundred degrees in the dark with the wind still on the far
side and the flame grey and the rate never once where you wanted it.
The kiln reaches temperature at twenty past eleven on the Saturday morning, seventeen hours after you
lit it, and the four big jars are at the back and you will not know about the glaze for a week.
-> ending_soak

== ending_held ==
You hold the rate through the wind and the kiln is at temperature by half past seven in the morning,
ten and a half hours after you lit it, and you brick up the spyhole and put the boards away and go in
and sleep for six hours in the middle of the day like a man who works nights, which is what a firing
makes of you.
On the following Friday you open it. The four jars are whole and the glaze on all four has run the way
it should, and two of the smaller ones have crazed, which is nothing, and you sit on the crate with
your hands black to the wrist and look at the biggest jar for a while, because it is the best thing you
have made this year and because you very nearly did not.
-> END

== the_cool ==
You brick up the spyhole at half past seven on the Saturday evening and the kiln takes three days to
come down, which it does, and you open it on the Tuesday afternoon with the door of the shed open on
a cold bright day.
The four big jars are whole. The glaze is a shade greener than the last firing, which is the wood and
not you, and it is the glaze you have been trying to get since 2019, and you carry the biggest one into
the house in both arms and put it on the kitchen table where the light comes in, and your hands are
shaking from the weight and from twenty-six hours and from the ordinary luck of a kiln that decided to
be kind.
-> END

== the_cool_slow ==
You were eleven hours getting to six hundred and then the wind took a third off the rate and you let
it, and the kiln was at temperature at twenty past seven on the Saturday evening, sixteen and a half
hours after you lit it, and you went to bed while it was still hot behind the bricks.
It comes down over three days and you open it on the Tuesday, and the four jars are whole and the glaze
is right - and you will never know which of the two things did it, the slow ramp or the long firing or
the nine hours of the night you spent sitting on a crate doing nothing, and there is no version of this
trade in which you get to find out.
-> END

== ending_soak ==
The glaze on the four jars is matte, which it should not be, and the reason is in the firing and the
firing is in your log in your own handwriting: eight hundred for three hours and then a slow climb
through the last four hundred with the wind on the far side.
You have made matte before and you will make it again and there is a market for it, and the biggest jar
goes on the shelf in the workshop rather than into the house, and you fire again in the March on a
still night and it comes out right, and the jar from the November stays where it is, on the shelf by
the door, where you can see it from the wheel.
-> END
